  <title>Diagonal lines</title>

  <p><gui>+ Diagonal</gui> adds a line from one corner of its box to the
  opposite one (<code>^GD</code> in ZPL). For a straight horizontal or
  vertical line, use a thin <link xref="frame#rule">frame</link>.</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Width</gui> and <gui>Height</gui></title>
      <p>The box the line crosses, in dots, 3 to 32000. The line always runs
      from corner to corner, so changing the width or height, or dragging a
      handle, changes how steep it is.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Direction</gui></title>
      <p><gui>Right-leaning ( / )</gui>, from bottom left to top right, or
      <gui>Left-leaning ( \ )</gui>, from top left to bottom right.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Thickness</gui></title>
      <p>Measured across the box, along each row of dots, rather than square
      to the line. So a shallow line looks thinner on paper than a steep one
      of the same thickness, and the line's ends are cut level with the top
      and bottom of the box. At the box's full width the line fills the box
      solid, so the thickness cannot go past that.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Colour</gui> and <gui>Reverse</gui></title>
      <p>As for a <link xref="frame">frame</link>.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <note>
    <p>Zebra's manual does not say which way a printer measures a
    diagonal's thickness. The canvas follows the rule above, which has not
    yet been checked against a printer. If a printer measures it square to
    the line instead, a shallow line prints thicker than the canvas
    shows.</p>
  </note>
</page>
